Credit bureaus and lenders treat **inconsistencies across documents as a red flag** because they often signal identity fraud, data-entry errors, or deliberate misrepresentation. When the same person’s name, address, date of birth, SSN/SIN, or employment details appear slightly different on a credit application, driver’s license, pay stub, or utility bill, it raises questions about whether:

• the applicant is who they claim to be (identity theft or synthetic identity);  
• supporting documents have been altered or forged;  
• the applicant is inflating income, hiding recent moves, or obscuring past defaults.

Even small mismatches—like a middle initial vs. full middle name, or a one-digit ZIP code error—can trigger extra verification steps or outright denial, because lenders are legally required to “know their customer” and face stiff penalties if they fund fraudulent accounts. Consistency across documents is therefore a quick, low-cost proxy for authenticity and risk.

They would enlarge the **thermal buffer**—the extra heat-sink capacity that sits between normal operation and the cell’s trigger temperature. That means:

- increasing the mass or surface area that can absorb heat (thicker heat spreaders, larger enclosure walls, metal frames bonded to cells);  
- widening the air or coolant channels so a partial blockage still leaves enough flow;  
- lowering the baseline temperature (larger inlet vents, lower ambient set-point, or active cooling that starts earlier);  
- adding phase-change or graphite pads that soak up transient spikes without letting any cell rise sharply.

Each of these steps adds joules of “headroom” per cell, so the same real-world variation in load, airflow, or ambient temperature produces a smaller temperature swing. The outcome becomes more predictable because the system has farther to travel before it reaches the failure threshold.
